A minimally better version reads the error for its actual content before deciding what to do:

```js
async function handleStepFailure(step, error, planAssumptions) {
  const contradicted = planAssumptions.find(a => a.contradictedBy(error));
  if (contradicted) {
    logger.warn(`Step ${step.id} failed — assumption invalidated: "${contradicted.text}"`);
    return { retryable: false, invalidatedAssumption: contradicted, error };
  }
  logger.warn(`Step ${step.id} failed (likely transient): ${error.message}`);
  return retryWithBackoff(() => executeStep(step));
}
```

This needs the plan's assumptions to actually be named (per the previous lesson) and each one to carry a way of recognizing when an error contradicts it — a real cost, but the payoff is a failure handler that can tell "try again, this resolves itself" apart from "stop retrying, something specific and stable changed," which is exactly the distinction the next two lessons build on.

