contentSections.body:
The fix on both sides of this lesson is the same discipline: a handoff should be the receiving agent's actual working material, structured and specific — not the sending agent's vibe about its own work (too little), and not the sending agent's entire process (too much). In practice this usually means a structured result object, not free-form prose either way:

```js
// Worker returns a structured result, not raw process
async function researchCompany(company) {
  const findings = await runResearchAgent(company); // internal process, tool calls, dead ends — stays internal
  return {
    company,
    facts: findings.extractedFacts,       // specific, sourced
    sources: findings.citedSources,
    confidence: findings.confidenceLevel,
    flagsForReview: findings.uncertainties, // named gaps, not silently dropped
  };
}
// The orchestrator passes this structured result to the synthesis agent —
// not the worker's full internal conversation.
```

Note the `flagsForReview` field: things the worker was genuinely uncertain about don't get silently dropped in the compression from process to result — they get named explicitly, so the receiving agent knows what it's not fully sure of, without having to read the whole process to find out.
